INT. FUNERAL HOME - FAMILY ROOM - AFTERNOON

Sour coffee. A sheet cake with a cut missing. Folding chairs facing a closed door.

DOLLY SUTHERLAND, 50s, pulls tissues from a box. RAY-RAY SUTHERLAND, 20s, tugs at a tie. UNCLE WENDELL, 70s, in a brown suit, pours coffee into a paper cup. AUNT PRISCILLA, 60s, in a lavender dress, adjusts a hat. CAROL BENNING, 40s, the neighbor, holds a covered dish.

DOLLY
She wanted the blue one.

PRISCILLA
She did not. She wanted the green.

RAY-RAY
Aunt Dolly said blue.

WENDELL
Blue is for babies.

PRISCILLA
Wendell, hush.

CAROL
I brought the casserole.

DOLLY
Carol, you shouldn't have.

CAROL
It's tuna.

WENDELL
Lord.

RAY-RAY
Does anyone know where she put the will?

PRISCILLA
Ray-Ray!

DOLLY
Not here.

RAY-RAY
Then where?

WENDELL
Not today, son.

CAROL
I'll just put this in the kitchen.

DOLLY
She was my mother, Priscilla.

PRISCILLA
She was my sister.

DOLLY
She was my mother first.

PRISCILLA
She was my sister longer.

They both stop. Their eyes meet. Dolly looks away first. Priscilla straightens her hat.

PRISCILLA (CONT'D)
The green one, then.

DOLLY
The green.
